<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bureau of Insular Affairs</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bureau of Insular Affairs was an agency of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> War <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> that oversaw the civil government of territories acquired after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish-American War</a>, chiefly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and, for a time, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>. Created in 1898 and given its name in 1902, it was dissolved in 1939, its work passing to the Interior Department.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bureau-of-insular-affairs.jpg" alt="Bureau of Insular Affairs" width="320" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:US-BureauOfInsularAffairs-Insignia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The bureau began on 13 December 1898 as the Division of Customs and Insular Affairs in the Office of the Secretary of War, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a> had transferred the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It supervised the customs and civil affairs of these areas. The word insular was already attached to Cuba and Puerto Rico, for which Spain had created autonomous insular governments in February 1898. The office became the Division of Insular Affairs in 1900 and the Bureau of Insular Affairs in 1902.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The bureau handled the civil side of the Philippine government from 1898 to 1935; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-military-government-of-the-philippine-islands">United States Military Government of the Philippine Islands</a> gave way to the Insular Government after the Spooner Amendment of 1901. It administered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, called Porto Rico in official documents until 1932, from 1898 to 1900 and again from 1909 to 1934, under the civil government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foraker-act">Foraker Act</a> of 1900 as amended by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jones-shafroth-act">Jones–Shafroth Act</a> of 1917. In 1915, following the Insular Cases, the Attorney General gave the opinion that these areas were unincorporated territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>Its oversight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> was never clearly defined, but from 1906 to 1909 it administered the sovereign Republic of Cuba during the Second Occupation of Cuba. It briefly oversaw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a> in 1904–1905, ran the Dominican customs receivership from 1905 to 1939 and the Haitian customs receivership from 1920 to 1924. In 1939 the Division of Territories and Island Possessions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of the Interior took its place; that division was later renamed the Office of Insular Affairs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The office was headed by army officers. Major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-j-pershing">John J. Pershing</a> led the Division of Customs and Insular Affairs in 1899, followed by Clarence R. Edwards, who remained chief after the change of name until 1912. Major General Frank McIntyre then served from 1912 to 1929, followed by Brigadier General Francis L. Parker, Brigadier General Creed F. Cox and Brigadier General Charles Burnett, whose term ended on 1 July 1939.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bureau of Insular Affairs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Philippine Organic Act of 1902 set its framework, under the <b>Bureau of Insular Affairs</b> in Washington.</blockquote><a href="/state/insular-government-of-the-philippine-islands">Insular Government of the Philippine Islands</a></li></ul></section>
